{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 56, :comments ["<b>Whether for many or for few.</b> In tractate Bava Batra (122a) they stated, and this is their language: It was raised as a question for them: Was the land of Israel divided among the tribes or by heads of men? Come and hear: ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״ (whether for many or for few). And it was further taught in a baraita: In the future the land of Israel is destined to be divided among thirteen tribes, for at first it was divided only among twelve, and so forth, and it was divided only by silver [i.e., by equal valuation], as it is stated: ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״. Rabbi Yehudah said: A se'ah in Judea is worth five se'ah in the Galilee — end of quote. And Rashbam wrote there, and this is his language: Come and hear ״בֵּין רַב״ and so forth — that each tribe takes whatever the lot gives it, whether it received a large portion, such as a tribe that had few people, or whether it had a small portion, such as one that had many people, and so forth, for all the tribes divide equally, and so forth — end of quote. And it is difficult in the words of the Gemara: why does it deduce from the verse, since there is no necessity [no compelling proof] from it, for in the baraita that it brings, which explains it with reference to near and far from Jerusalem — near is called רַב (much), far מְעָט (little), because it is more valuable [per unit area]." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it appears that the words of the Talmud contradict their end with their beginning, for from its statement ״Come and hear: בֵּין רַב״ and so forth, it reveals its opinion that it holds the land was divided equally among the tribes, whereas from its statement ״And it was further taught״ this is not implied, for there it explains the phrase ״בֵּין רַב״ and so forth as referring to a far location and a near location. And from this we are compelled to say that the Talmud holds that the tanna [of the baraita] regarding the future division of the land of Israel holds that the land of Israel was divided among the tribes equally, and therefore the Talmud noted in its statement ״Come and hear: בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״ to say that it holds in accordance with the baraita that it was divided among the tribes equally. And these words are not sound, for if we interpret ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״ as between a tribe that takes much and one that takes little according to the number of its people, whence do we derive to speak of far location and near location? And likewise the reverse: if we interpret it as far location and so forth, whence do we derive to say that it was divided equally, and so forth?" "<b>Furthermore,</b> if we say that it was divided equally among the tribes, then we would establish the dispute between Rabbi Yonatan and Rabbi Yoshiya regarding the division of each and every tribe individually — one master [holds it was divided] according to those who left Egypt, and one master according to those who entered the land. If so, what does the Gemara (Bava Batra 117a) ask, and this is its language: According to the one who says [it was divided] according to those who left Egypt, that is why the children of Joseph cried out, as it is written (Joshua 17:14): ״וַיְדַבְּרוּ בְּנֵי יוֹסֵף״; but according to the one who says [it was divided] according to those who entered the land, what are they crying about? They are all equal for the children [i.e., the minors] — end of quote. I see no difficulty, since according to all opinions the children of Joseph took equally with the [other] tribes, and the claim of the children of Joseph is that they are more numerous than all the tribes, and whether according to the one who says [it was divided] according to those who left Egypt or according to the one who says according to those who entered the land, their dispute concerns the division within the tribe itself. Rather, it is certain that both according to Rabbi Yonatan and according to Rabbi Yoshiya the land was not divided equally among the tribes, and one should not posit that the tanna of the baraita holds the opposite of both of them. Furthermore, if we say that it was divided among the tribes equally, the tribe of Judah should have cried out all the more, for according to the number of those who entered the land, the count of Judah is seventy-six thousand five hundred, and the count of Manasseh and Ephraim together is eighty-five thousand two hundred — so it turns out that both of them exceed Judah by only eight thousand seven hundred, yet they took double, for they took two portions while Judah took one, as is explained there, that [the land] was divided only among twelve tribes besides Levi, who took no portion in the land." "<b>Rather,</b> the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it never entered the mind of the Gemara to say that [the land] was divided among the tribes equally, according to the words of Rashbam. And the explanation of its words, when it said ״Come and hear: בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״ — from the implication of the verse it proves that the division was among the tribes, for from the fact that it mentioned regarding the lot ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״, we see that the division that took place by lot included a large portion and a small portion. And if you should say [it was divided] by heads of men, it would not be appropriate to say ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט,״ and the meaning of רַב and מְעָט is between a tribe that has many people according to its numbers and a tribe that has few people according to its numbers — everything would be by lot with respect to the location where they would take their portions. And its statement ״And it was further taught: In the future the land of Israel״ and so forth — it further intends to bring the matter in greater clarification, for the disputant has grounds to object and say that one cannot prove from the verse ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט״ except to say that it was not divided by heads of men, but one may still doubt whether [it was divided] among families or among tribes. Therefore it said ״And it was further taught: In the future the land of Israel״ and so forth — it was divided among twelve tribes. We thus say that the meaning of ״בֵּין רַב לִמְעָט,״ according to all opinions, does not imply from it that the tribes divided equally; rather, they divided only according to the number of those counted, in accordance with the words of the tanna of the Sifrei that I cited under the verse ״אִישׁ לְפִי פְקֻדָיו״ (each man according to his counted ones)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:19.771432258Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}